  'tshwane-level-2-water-restrictions-2026-11-13',
  'Tshwane imposes Level 2 water restrictions as demand outstrips supply',
  'Utilities', '2026-11-13', 'Rekord', 'https://www.citizen.co.za/rekord/news-headlines/2026/11/13/level-2-water-restrictions-imposed-as-consumption-exceeds-allocation/',
  'The City of Tshwane has brought in Level 2 water restrictions and higher tariffs after consumption outstripped its Rand Water allocation.',
  'The City of Tshwane has put Level 2 water restrictions into effect, metro spokesperson Lindela Mashigo said, after months of rising demand and falling storage in the Rand Water system that supplies the capital. In September, the city used about 34% more water than its permanent Rand Water licence allows and 19% more than its temporary allocation, prompting Rand Water to ask Tshwane to cut daily consumption by roughly 200 megalitres.

Under the restrictions, households and businesses may not use municipal drinking water on gardens or lawns, to wash cars with a hosepipe, or to fill swimming pools. Residents have been asked to fix leaking taps and pipes, cut back on shower time, reuse greywater where they can, and notify the city of any leaks or bursts they notice. The restrictions also bring in the city''s approved Level 2 tariffs, charged by consumption band, though qualifying indigent households keep 12 kilolitres of free water each month and a network access charge exemption applies to properties valued at R250,000 or less.

The restrictions are set to run for an initial 30 days, after which the city will weigh them against supply and demand before deciding whether to extend, ease or tighten them further. Compliance is compulsory, and the by-law enforcement unit of the Tshwane Metro Police Department will step up monitoring. Mashigo described water security as a joint duty for residents, businesses and institutions, and said every litre saved helps stabilise the supply system.',
  NULL, NULL, NULL,
  '["https://www.citizen.co.za/rekord/news-headlines/2026/11/13/level-2-water-restrictions-imposed-as-consumption-exceeds-allocation/", "https://www.jacarandafm.com/news/news/tshwane-implements-restrictions-as-consumption-exceeds/"]',
  'agent'
);

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO news (
  slug, title, category, published_date, source_name, source_url,
  summary, body, image_url, image_credit, image_source, verification_json, source
) VALUES (
  'bangladesh-proteas-centurion-second-test-2026-11-15',
  'Bangladesh and Proteas head to Centurion for second Test',
  'Sport', '2026-11-15', 'The Citizen', 'https://www.citizen.co.za/sport/cricket/south-africa-bangladesh-day-one-highlights/',
  'After a tense opening day in Johannesburg, South Africa and Bangladesh move to SuperSport Park for a Test from 23 to 27 November.',
  'Bangladesh finished day one on 327 for 6 after winning the toss and batting first in the series opener at the Wanderers in Johannesburg. Towhid Hridoy made 137 before adding 206 for the sixth wicket with Litton Das, who was unbeaten on 92 at the close. Kagiso Rabada took 5 for 51 for the hosts, dismissing debutant Mahfijul Islam Robin with the very first delivery of the match.

The five-day match, which runs from 15 to 19 November, is the first of two Tests between the sides in this year''s ICC World Test Championship cycle. Once it finishes, both teams travel to Pretoria for the second Test, scheduled for 23 to 27 November at SuperSport Park in Centurion.

South Africa go into the series on top of the ICC''s Test rankings after beating Australia, while Bangladesh sit fifth in the championship standings. Captain Temba Bavuma leads a squad that includes the returning Anrich Nortje, Tony de Zorzi and Zubayr Hamza. Coach Shukri Conrad has said the pace-friendly conditions typical of Highveld pitches suit his seam bowling options.',
  NULL, NULL, NULL,
  '["https://www.citizen.co.za/sport/cricket/south-africa-bangladesh-day-one-highlights/", "https://www.icc-cricket.com/tournaments/world-test-championship/news/pacer-back-for-south-africa-s-next-wtc27-assignment"]',
  'agent'
);
